  "slug": "condensation-dynamics-e624",
  "summary": "Four preprints in one week use polymer models to infer chromosome ensembles, dynamics and loop extrusion, while testing the limits of microscopy-based looping estimates.",
  "synthesis": "The common thread is the use of polymer models to link chromosome structure, dynamics and mechanism. One preprint builds epigenetic-state-informed ensembles that reproduce Hi-C maps across genetic and epigenetic contexts. Another, the Full-Inversion Chromatin model, learns an effective force field from Hi-C contacts to infer yeast chromosome ensembles and locus-dependent dynamics across the cell cycle. A mesoscale jump-diffusion model of cohesin extrusion, parametrized from single-molecule data, addresses stall force, kinetic phases and CTCF barrier function. A ground-truth simulation study shows that thresholding noisy 3D microscopy distances misestimates looping probability, lifetime and search time. The new elements are dynamical inference from static contact data and explicit validation of how well experimental readouts constrain looping.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-sonnet-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-13"
}
